.TH SMITHPROXY-TOPOLOGY 7 "OCT 2026" "Smithproxy" "Miscellaneous Information Manual"
.SH NAME
smithproxy-topology \- deployment patterns for Smithproxy traffic interception
.SH DESCRIPTION
Smithproxy can be placed inline as a transparent router, run on the observed
host, or serve explicit proxy clients. The correct design depends on who owns
routing, whether original addresses must be preserved, and whether applications
can be configured to use a proxy.
.PP
The principal choices are:
.nf
    routed traffic       -> TPROXY
    local host traffic   -> REDIRECT
    proxy-aware clients  -> SOCKS4/5 or HTTP CONNECT
    isolated workloads   -> namespace/container routing to TPROXY
.fi
.PP
Linux packet diversion only delivers traffic to Smithproxy. Forward and return
routes, loop prevention, certificate trust and policy remain separate parts of
the deployment.
.SH INLINE ROUTER WITH TPROXY
The preferred transparent topology places Smithproxy on the routed path between
clients and their destinations:
.nf
    client network
          |
          v
    +------------------------+
    | Linux router           |
    | mangle/TPROXY          |
    | Smithproxy listeners   |
    +------------------------+
          |
          v
    upstream network
.fi
.PP
TPROXY preserves the original destination without rewriting it in the NAT
table. Policy routing delivers marked packets locally to the configured
Smithproxy TCP, TLS, UDP or QUIC listener.
.PP
The host must have IP forwarding enabled and routes toward both client and
upstream networks. When a policy preserves the original client source address,
the server return path must traverse the same Smithproxy host. Otherwise the
server can reply directly to the client, bypassing the second proxy leg and
breaking the connection.
.SS Advantages
.IP \(bu 2
No proxy configuration is required in client applications.
.IP \(bu 2
The original destination is available to policy and routing.
.IP \(bu 2
TCP and UDP can be intercepted, including a separate QUIC path.
.IP \(bu 2
One router can cover multiple client networks.
.SS Risks
.IP \(bu 2
Asymmetric routing causes hard-to-diagnose timeouts.
.IP \(bu 2
Broad diversion rules can intercept management traffic or Smithproxy's own
upstream connections.
.IP \(bu 2
Transparent source addresses require additional routing and kernel support.
.SH SAME-HOST REDIRECT
REDIRECT is suitable when the application and Smithproxy run in the same
network namespace:
.nf
    +--------------------------------+
    | one Linux host                 |
    |                                |
    | application -> OUTPUT/REDIRECT |
    |                    |           |
    |                    v           |
    |                Smithproxy      |
    +--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
Rules in the NAT OUTPUT path redirect selected local TCP connections to the
Smithproxy REDIRECT listeners. Exclude the Smithproxy service identity, marks or
other distinguishing properties so that its upstream connections are not
redirected back into itself.
.PP
TCP original destinations can be recovered. General local UDP destinations are
not retained by REDIRECT, so same-host UDP interception is primarily useful for
DNS sent to a configured resolver. Use TPROXY in a separate namespace or routed
topology when arbitrary UDP destinations matter.
.SH EXPLICIT SOCKS PROXY
Applications can connect directly to the Smithproxy SOCKS listener:
.nf
    application -- SOCKS4/5 --> Smithproxy --> destination
.fi
.PP
SOCKS avoids kernel interception rules and asymmetric routing. SOCKS5 clients
can supply a hostname, allowing Smithproxy to resolve it and apply name-aware
policy. Applications should enable proxy-side DNS resolution when they must not
resolve names locally.
.PP
The upstream connection originates from the Smithproxy host. Explicit clients
do not obtain transparent source-address preservation. UDP ASSOCIATE support is
more limited than ordinary TCP CONNECT use and should be tested with the target
client.
.SH EXPLICIT HTTP CONNECT
HTTP/1.0 and HTTP/1.1 clients can request a tunnel through the configured
CONNECT listener:
.nf
    application -- CONNECT host:port --> Smithproxy --> destination
.fi
.PP
After the tunnel is established, TLS detection and policy processing follow the
normal TCP path. IPv4, bracketed IPv6 and hostname authorities are accepted.
Clients must wait for the successful CONNECT response before sending tunneled
application data; request pipelining is not supported.
.PP
CONNECT is convenient for browsers and tools with an HTTP proxy setting. SOCKS
is more general for non-HTTP TCP applications.
.SH NETWORK NAMESPACES
Namespaces provide a clean transparent lab or single-host deployment without
REDIRECT limitations:
.nf
    +-------------------+       veth       +-------------------+
    | workload netns    |------------------| proxy/root netns  |
    | default via veth  |                  | TPROXY + policy   |
    +-------------------+                  | Smithproxy        |
                                           +-------------------+
.fi
.PP
Give the workload namespace a default route through its veth peer. Apply TPROXY
rules on the receiving interface in the proxy namespace and add the required
packet-mark policy route to the local table. This preserves TCP and UDP original
destinations while keeping Smithproxy's upstream sockets outside the intercepted
namespace.
.PP
Namespaces are useful for integration tests because routes, firewall state,
DNS and captures can be created and destroyed without changing the host's main
network. They also make it easier to prove that the client has no bypass path.
.SH DOCKER AND OTHER CONTAINERS
Container traffic can be routed through Smithproxy in several ways.
.SS Smithproxy on the host
Run application containers on a bridge whose egress reaches host firewall
rules. Divert packets arriving from that bridge to host Smithproxy TPROXY
listeners. Bridge netfilter settings determine whether bridged traffic visits
the expected iptables/nftables hooks; verify them instead of assuming the host
rules see every packet.
.SS Smithproxy container with host networking
A Smithproxy container using host networking can bind the host listener ports
and share the host routing/firewall setup. It needs the capabilities and mounted
configuration, certificates, logs and capture directories required by the
deployment. Host networking reduces namespace isolation and should be treated
as privileged infrastructure.
.SS Dedicated gateway container or namespace
Place Smithproxy in a gateway namespace/container and set application
containers' default route to it. This resembles the inline TPROXY topology and
keeps interception rules scoped to the gateway. Container orchestrators may
recreate interfaces and routes, so apply rules after the relevant network is
present and make setup idempotent.
.PP
Avoid relying only on container DNS settings when the goal is enforcement; an
application can connect to an address directly unless routing and egress policy
also require the gateway.
.SH KUBERNETES AND ORCHESTRATORS
Smithproxy can be used as a node-level gateway or as a dedicated routed proxy
for selected workloads. A generic sidecar is not automatically transparent:
the pod network namespace still needs REDIRECT or TPROXY rules and loop
exclusions.
.PP
Account for the CNI plugin, service NAT, network policy, pod churn and health
checks. Preserve a management path which is not intercepted. Validate source
and destination addresses after every NAT layer before writing Smithproxy
policy around them.
.SH QUIC AND UDP
Generic UDP and QUIC use different Smithproxy listeners. QUIC interception must
preserve the original destination port and is normally installed with TPROXY
using an unchanged on-port value. When the experimental QUIC listener is
disabled, blocking UDP on configured QUIC service ports prevents HTTP/3 from
bypassing TCP/TLS inspection.
.PP
Test DNS, generic UDP and QUIC independently. A working TCP topology does not
prove that packet marks, local routes and firewall hooks are correct for UDP.
.SH IPV4 AND IPV6
Build equivalent interception and policy-routing paths for both address
families. An IPv4-only rule set can leave IPv6 as an uninspected bypass. Confirm
that listener binding, local routes, forwarding, return routes and policy
objects cover both families.
.PP
If a feature or kernel path is not available for one family, block or route that
traffic deliberately rather than allowing silent fallback outside Smithproxy.
.SH LOOP PREVENTION
Never send Smithproxy's upstream connection back to the same interception rule.
Common exclusions use one or more of:
.IP \(bu 2
the Smithproxy service user or cgroup;
.IP \(bu 2
a firewall mark set before upstream routing;
.IP \(bu 2
the listener port and ingress interface;
.IP \(bu 2
a separate network namespace or routing table.
.PP
A loop often appears as repeated local connections, rapidly increasing session
counts or timeouts without packets leaving the host.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Application using SOCKS5 with proxy-side DNS
.nf
    curl --socks5-hostname proxy.example:1080 https://example.net/
.fi
.PP
The hostname is sent to Smithproxy instead of being resolved by curl. This is a
quick way to test explicit policy and TLS inspection without changing routing.
.SS Application using HTTP CONNECT
.nf
    curl --proxy http://proxy.example:3128 https://example.net/
.fi
.PP
The client opens a CONNECT tunnel and then performs TLS through it. The client
must trust the Smithproxy CA when the matching policy enables TLS inspection.
.SS Routed namespace lab
.nf
    ip netns add app
    ip link add app-veth type veth peer name proxy-veth
    ip link set app-veth netns app
    ip address add 192.0.2.1/30 dev proxy-veth
    ip link set proxy-veth up
    ip netns exec app ip address add 192.0.2.2/30 dev app-veth
    ip netns exec app ip link set app-veth up
    ip netns exec app ip link set lo up
    ip netns exec app ip route add default via 192.0.2.1
.fi
.PP
Apply Smithproxy TPROXY and packet-mark policy-routing rules to traffic arriving
on
.B proxy-veth .
The exact firewall commands depend on the configured listener ports and whether
iptables or nftables owns the host rules. Remove the namespace after testing.
.SS Container gateway
.nf
    application containers -> bridge/veth -> gateway namespace
                                      TPROXY -> Smithproxy
                                              -> upstream route
.fi
.PP
Set the application network's default route to the gateway, then verify that
bridge traffic reaches the intended firewall hooks. Keep Smithproxy's upstream
interface outside the intercepted bridge to avoid a loop.
.SH CERTIFICATE AND HOST-KEY TRUST
Routing traffic through Smithproxy is not enough for TLS or SSH MITM. TLS
clients must trust the Smithproxy CA. SSH clients must accept the host key
presented by the configured SSH profile. Deploy trust material through managed
client configuration; do not solve trust errors by globally disabling
verification.
.SH MANAGEMENT PLANE
Keep SSH administration of the proxy host, routing protocols, monitoring, CLI
and HTTP API access outside broad interception rules. Use a dedicated interface,
address range, namespace or explicit early bypass rule. Test management access
before applying persistent firewall changes remotely.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
Check the topology from the outside inward:
.IP 1. 3
Does the client route the packet through the intended host or namespace?
.IP 2. 3
Does the expected firewall hook count the packet?
.IP 3. 3
Does the packet receive the expected mark and local policy route?
.IP 4. 3
Is the corresponding Smithproxy listener active?
.IP 5. 3
Which policy rule and profiles match the session?
.IP 6. 3
Does the upstream socket leave through the correct route without looping?
.IP 7. 3
Does return traffic traverse Smithproxy?
.PP
Useful commands include:
.nf
    show status
    diag proxy session list
    diag proxy policy list
    diag proxy quic list
    ip rule show
    ip route show table all
    ip -6 rule show
    ip -6 route show table all
.fi
.SH DEPLOYMENT CHECKLIST
.IP \(bu 2
Choose TPROXY, REDIRECT or an explicit proxy intentionally.
.IP \(bu 2
Exclude management and Smithproxy-originated traffic.
.IP \(bu 2
Verify forward and return routes before enabling transparent source addresses.
.IP \(bu 2
Cover both IPv4 and IPv6 or block the unused family.
.IP \(bu 2
Handle QUIC explicitly rather than allowing accidental HTTP/3 bypass.
.IP \(bu 2
Deploy TLS CA and SSH host-key trust to intended clients.
.IP \(bu 2
Start with a narrow test policy and inspect live sessions before expanding it.
.SH TESTING POLICY
Since October 2026, Smithproxy follows a strict documented testing policy with
commit-specific reports under
.IR docs/test-reports/ .
Topology validation should record the ingress mode, address families, firewall
path, forward and return routes, expected bypasses and tested failure cases.
